    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d401">

                <pb facs="0666" n="652" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg652"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.CC/7–1544</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Roosevelt to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi><anchor n="19" xml:id="d401-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Aboard the Presidential Special</hi></placeName>,] <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-15">July 15, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I have read the memorandum<anchor n="20" xml:id="d401-f2"/> with great interest
                    and it has my approval. I like the plans for discussion and the preliminary
                    outline of the proposals for a general international organization. Good
                    luck!</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Franklin</hi> D. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Roosevelt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d401-f1" n="19" type="source" xml:id="d401-f1-content">
                <p>President Roosevelt had left Washington aboard the Presidential Special on July
                    13 and returned on August 17. For an itinerary of this trip, see <ref
                        target="frus1945Malta#pg_3">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Conferences at Malta and
                        Yalta, p. 3</ref>, footnote 3.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d401-f2" n="20" xml:id="d401-f2-content">
                <p>Reference is made to both the memorandum of July 11, <ref target="#pg647">p.
                        647</ref>, on recommended arrangements for the Dumbarton Oaks Conversations
                    and the July 6 draft of Tentative Proposals handed to President Roosevelt on
                    July 12. The final draft of the “Possible Plan” of July 6, with one exception,
                    the submission of the section regarding international trusteeship, was identical
                    with that transmitted after approval by the President on July 15, to the United
                    Kingdom, the Soviet Union, and China on July 18, 1944, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >infra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
